# Importer fixture for scripts/check/check-use-warning-oracle-deployed.shs.
# Provider: src/lib/common/use_warning_probe.spl (reached as std.common.*).
#
# One braced group import naming three kinds of symbol, so a single run yields
# all three of the check's assertions:
#   uw_probe_plain_provided  — ordinary `fn`  -> oracle must be SILENT (control)
#   uw_probe_extern_provided — `extern fn`    -> oracle must be SILENT (FP guard)
#   uw_probe_zzz_absent      — declared nowhere -> oracle MUST warn (positive)
#
# DO NOT wrap this in `fn main()`. Doing so makes `simple run` drop the
# diagnostic entirely: the fixture still prints and still exits 0, but the
# `[use-warning]` line disappears and the check reads as a regression. This was
# observed directly while building the check — the same shape as the known
# "`fn main` makes `run` drop every describe/it" defect. Top-level statements
# keep the module-load path that the oracle hangs off.
#
# The braced form is also required: `warn_unprovided_use_names` returns early
# for anything that is not a `{...}` group import.

use std.common.use_warning_probe.{uw_probe_plain_provided, uw_probe_extern_provided, uw_probe_zzz_absent}

print("use-warning-probe-ran")
print(uw_probe_plain_provided())
